  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe MCV which caused more widespread heavy rain has moved east\nof the region. However, low-level convergence continues on the \nstalled boundary along the I-70 corridor, along with \nprecipitable water values still in the 1.6 to 1.8 inch range.\nThis is continuing to create bands of moderate to heavy rain\nalong and south of the boundary south of I-70. The various CAMs\nsuggest this activity will continue through 22Z or so, tapering\noff from north to south as the boundary gets a push from a\nshortwave crossing the Upper Ohio Valley. With much of the\nongoing Flood Watch already covered by warnings and with a\nrelatively low threat of new flooding, will let the Watch expire\nat 2 PM. Quieter weather takes hold after sunset with the \narrival of surface high pressure, with fog likely to develop \novernight where the sky can clear.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...\nWeak surface high pressure still tries to build south on Friday.\nModel guidance appears a bit less bullish on shower/storm \ndevelopment during the afternoon. Moisture depth/subsidence- \ninduced capping may largely suppress development at least north \nof the PA turnpike, and potentially further south than that. Will\nkeep NBM suggestion of PoPs south of I-70/east of I-77, although\nsome minor modification may be needed with future model runs.\n\nThe next 500mb shortwave trough pivots across the Great Lakes\nlater Friday night/Saturday morning, and lingers across the\nUpper Ohio Valley into Sunday with additional vorticity maxima\nmaintaining lower heights. An associated frontal boundary looks\nto swing through during late afternoon/evening on Saturday,\nalthough the timing still is questionable. There are still\nscenarios, given favorable timing/clearing, that show sufficient\nCAPE and DCAPE for a downburst wind threat. The 12Z RRFS, for\nexample, does show up to 1500 J/kg CAPE/800-1000 J/kg DCAPE in\nareas east of Pittsburgh during the afternoon that could support\nthe severe threat. Much of the available machine-learning \nguidance does continue to highlight the potential. Still, \nchanges in frontal timing or cloud cover could reduce the \noverall threat, and future CAMs runs will be evaluated to\nreassess the risk.\n\nThat then serves as a precursor to additional shortwave trough\nmovement aloft combining with a more defined surface cold front\nthat passes through sometime Saturday afternoon into Sunday.\nVariances in upper trough depth/positioning and timing of the\nwave makes it difficult to assess potential storm environments\nand potential hazardous weather threats. Ensemble guidance,\ndriven primarily by Canadian modeling, does show scenarios with\nenough CAPE/dCAPE for a damaging wind threat (though weak shear\nwon't be a boon) that seems to be captured by a few other \nmachine-learning convective outlook models. This scenario is \nmainly tied to clean heating prior to the front/shortwave and \nSaturday afternoon/early evening storm occurrence; earlier/later\narrivals or more cloud cover is just as likely that would \ndampen or completely squash that risk. Trends will be monitored \nto understand how things may track. Scattered showers/storms\nremain possible on Sunday, but convective parameters appear a\nlittle less favorable for a severe threat.\n\nEnsembles continue to lend confidence to a drier forecast for\nthe first part of next week, as surface high pressure returns\nunder a weakening troughing pattern.",
